CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=00f1fcdb-0f068baf-010f-068baf83-00000004:None
CREATED:20251229T211123Z
DESCRIPTION:The "Frontiers in International Security" workshop hosted by t
 he Department of Political Science features short presentations from 10 e
 arly career scholars who are doing exciting research on a wide range of s
 ecurity-related topics. Duke faculty\, postdoctoral fellows\, and graduat
 e students will provide comments. Some interesting questions that present
 ers will speak to include: \n\n•Does decline in national power influence 
 a state's ability to make credible threats?\n\n•Are AI models deployed ac
 ross national security contexts biased in their assessments of political 
 violence?\n\n•Does real-time news about U.S. foreign policy decisions aff
 ect global public support for cooperation with the United States?\n\n•Why
  do states and their citizens often fight over barren\, seemingly worthle
 ss territories while not fighting over territories or issues that can be 
 potentially more valuable?\n\nThe workshop will run from 9:00am-4:15pm in
  Gross Hall 270. Breakfast (8:30-9:00) and lunch (12:15-1:00) are availab
 le to registered participants.\n \nFor questions\, please contact the fac
 ulty organizers: Rachel Myrick (rachel.myrick@duke.edu) and Kyle Beardsle
 y (kyle.beardsley@duke.edu).
DURATION:PT7H15M
DTSTAMP:20260409T160156Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260417T090000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260409T160156Z
